    vendor/renderer/version, a core GLES 2.0 read) and classifies it
    -- discrete, integrated, virtual, software rasterizer, unknown --
    as pure table-driven logic, exhaustively unit-pinned. The
    doctrine: Auto refuses a CPU-rasterizer GL context (llvmpipe,
    swrast, softpipe, SwiftShader -- the no-graphics-card machine or
    the failed-driver fallback) in favor of the specialized software
    backend, with the reason in the one-line startup report; --renderer
    gl still forces the GL path over any context (the operator is the
    final authority); an unrecognized GPU classifies as Unknown and
    counts as hardware -- never a silent degrade. The startup line
    names the device: "renderer: gles (hardware: EGL + GLES 2.0
    context up, discrete GPU: AMD Radeon ...)".
  * The sized output doctrine (--resolution WxH): the bring-up and
    every hotplug migration prefer a mode of exactly the forced size
    (preferred flag, then the highest refresh among matches); a size
    no connected connector offers is the typed startup failure
    listing every size the panel does offer (the operator's menu --
    never a silent nearest-neighbor guess); a migration to a display
    without the size falls back to the unsized doctrine and the world
    keeps the operator's preference for the next display that offers
    it. OutputGlobal::select_size re-points the protocol face (the
    current_mode flag, the geometry, the renderer's output
    description) at the forced mode; Mode::panel_4k60 joins the
    fixtures (594 MHz over 4400x2250 -- exactly 60.000 Hz).
  * The desktop matrix benchmark suite (the permanent ldp-bench
    rows): the Liquid desktop frame -- wallpaper, four rounded and
    shadowed windows in the quadrants, one frosted dock bar -- at
    1920x1080, 2560x1440, 3440x1440 (the 21:9 ultrawide), and
    3840x2160, at the High tier and at the tier the no-GPU doctrine
    picks for each size, steady state and (at the two ends) the cold
    first frame. Measured (5-run medians): High steady 7.31 / 11.49 /
    15.11 / 24.67 ms across the four sizes -- 60 Hz holds at High
    through the ultrawide; the doctrine's tier 5.96 / 7.95 / 10.96 /
    18.99 ms. The first run caught a real regression: the 4K High
    steady state measured 194 ms because the shadow memo's fixed
    6 Mi-word budget sat under a 4K desktop's working set (~6.5 Mi
    words) and the LRU evicted and rebuilt every material every
    frame; the budget now scales with the output (twice its pixels,
    floored at the phone era's 6 Mi so Phase 29 behavior is
    byte-exact) and 4K High dropped to 24.67 ms (7.9x, same bytes),
    pinned by the flat-rebuild-counter oracle.
  * docs/comparison.md: the honest field-by-field comparison against
    Wayland, X11/Xorg, macOS WindowServer, and Windows DWM -- twenty
    fields scored out of 100 with the reasoning attached, the
    measured spine (the desktop matrix, the phone frame, the 19 MiB
    peak-RSS bring-up), and every gap named with its roadmap line.
  * Tests (+25 new tests, 1,839 total as the gate measures on this rig): the classification table (every
    known renderer string, the software-wins-over-everything rule,
    the honest Unknown default); the identity summary and truncation;
    the mode-size parsing and rejection; the sized selection (the
    best exact match, the offered-menu failure, the no-display arm);
    the class-aware policy matrix (Auto+llvmpipe refused with the
    reason, forced-GL-over-llvmpipe honored, unknown/virtual stay
    hardware); resolution_session.rs -- the end-to-end exit
    criterion: the forced size that is offered serves it (the world,
    the flagged current mode, the scanout pixels), the honest miss
    fails with the menu, the migration honors the forced size and
    falls back honestly without dying; the budget-scaling semantics
    (the floor default, raise-never-lower, the miss/hit build
    counter) and the 4K thrash oracles (the flat
    shadow-rebuilds counter across a 4K desktop's steady frames;
    the scaled budget keeping a large working set resident where
    the floor evicts).
  * Release discipline 0.8.0 -> 0.9.0: workspace + lock + debian
    changelog/control + README (status/matrix/docs links/Phase 30
    bullet) + CHANGELOG 0.9.0 + roadmap (Phase 30 section, milestone
    v0.9.0) + maturity (v0.9.0, row 30) + benchmarks.md (the desktop
    matrix rows) + user-guide 3.10 (the desktop operator's section)
    + admin-guide + packaging README.

