lion-display (0.8.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Milestone release v0.8.0 -- the steady-state pass (Phase 29):
    the Liquid visual engine at 60 Hz on a weak CPU. The phone-frame
    benchmark (a 1080x2340 output, a plain wallpaper, four rounded
    and shadowed app cards, one frosted translucent panel, full
    damage every frame) measured 178.6 ms per frame at Phase 28 --
    5.6 fps; it now measures 8.70 ms steady state (20.5x) and
    64.8 ms first frame (2.75x), same machine, byte-identical
    output. The wins: shadows memoized as imagery (built once per
    unique params/geometry, LRU-bounded, honest over-budget
    recompute), frost memoized on the backdrop's own words (a full
    comparison, no hashing -- rebuilt only when what sits beneath
    actually changed), the corner-arc SDF band-limited to the arcs
    (pixel centers sit exactly half a pixel from every straight
    edge, so only the arcs ever produce fractional coverage -- 99.6%
    of a phone window's pixels never pay the sqrt again), the box
    blur rebuilt (edge-free interior loops, 16-column strip walk for
    cache-line-friendly vertical passes, an exact 48-bit reciprocal
    multiply for the window mean -- exhaustively proven equal to the
    division), word-level snapshot and material application, the
    copy path broadened to same-family formats (an XRGB window over
    an ARGB output word-copies), and the GL backend's frost readback
    and shadow textures served from the same memos with one retained
    readback buffer. Every equivalence is pinned by new oracle
    suites (the Phase 27 kernels restated verbatim as references and
    cross-checked over randomized shapes, including the degenerate
    capsule radii and the overlapping corner bands the corpus
    caught), and the steady-state transparency oracle: a warm
    renderer's frames stay byte- and stat-identical to a cold
    render's.

