lion-display (0.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Milestone release v0.6.0 -- the Liquid visual engine (Phase 27):
    the macOS-class material language on Linux, built for low-end
    devices. Three effects make the vocabulary -- antialiased rounded
    corners (an IEEE-exact SDF coverage), soft shadows (a blurred,
    ink-holed silhouette), and frosted translucency (the backdrop
    blurred, desaturated and veiled behind a glass pane) -- all pure
    integer-deterministic kernels shared by the software renderer and
    the GL path, so styled frames are byte-equal across backends (the
    layer-major Liquid GL stream, the upload-time coverage fold, the
    readback frost). The low-end doctrine ships with it: four quality
    tiers (minimal / low / medium / high) resolve from the machine
    through --effects auto -- a GL renderer serves the full 3-pass
    blur, a software renderer scales to the output's pixel budget, a
    tint veil keeps the identity where blur would stutter. The spring
    engine adds the motion vocabulary: critically-damped, fixed-substep
    integration, bit-reproducible -- the macOS feel. Also fixed: the
    GL stream's latent overlapping-damage double-composite (disjoint
    rect normalization in the damage clip).

